-- title:
--   Lower bound on the cone gap for points of the half-aperture cone
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ with $\lVert v\rVert = 1$, and let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$ with $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$. If $h \in E$ lies in the cone $\tilde V(v, \vartheta/2)$ of axis $v$ and apex angle $\vartheta/2$, then
--
--   $$\lVert h \rVert \, \sin\!\left(\frac{\vartheta}{2}\right) \;\le\; \mathrm{gap}(v, \vartheta, h),$$
--
--   where $\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,h) = \langle v,h\rangle \sin\vartheta - \lVert h - \langle v,h\rangle v\rVert\cos\vartheta$ is the signed distance from $h$ to the boundary of the cone of aperture $\vartheta$.
--
--   This is the quantitative content of "halving the apex angle buys room proportional to the distance": a point that lies in the narrower cone is at distance at least $\lVert h\rVert \sin(\vartheta/2)$ from the boundary of the wider one. Combined with the fact that a ball of radius below the gap lies inside the cone, it is what lets a whole cube of side comparable to $\lVert h \rVert$ be absorbed when the apex angle is relaxed from $\vartheta/2$ to $\vartheta$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The bound is stated with $\sin(\vartheta/2)$, not the sharp value $\sin(\vartheta - \angle(v,h))$; it is the worst case over the admissible angles. The hypotheses $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$ are needed for the sine and cosine signs.
-- source:
